# Project Consistency

## Objective

New code should look like it was written by the same team that wrote the rest of the project.

## Core Principles

- Match existing naming conventions (camelCase vs snake_case, file naming, folder structure).
- Use the same libraries/utilities the project already depends on instead of introducing equivalents.
- Follow existing error-handling and logging conventions.
- Match existing test structure and framework, don't introduce a second one.

## Requirements

- Check an existing sibling file for conventions before creating a new one from scratch.
- Reuse existing config, constants, and types instead of redefining them locally.
- Keep commit-sized changes consistent with how the project organizes similar changes.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Using a different date/HTTP/testing library than the one already in package.json/requirements.txt.
- Introducing a new naming scheme 'because it's cleaner' without updating the rest of the codebase.
- Mixing formatting styles within the same file.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ New files follow the same naming/structure as comparable existing files.
✓ No new dependency was added when an existing one already covers the need.
✓ Style and conventions are indistinguishable from the surrounding code.

## Golden Rule

If you can't tell which lines were written by the AI, consistency was achieved.
